[2026-04-14T07:54:50.100Z] [INFO]         "type": "tool_result",
[2026-04-14T07:54:50.100Z] [INFO]         "content": "1\t# Contributing to doublets-rs\n2\t\n3\tThank you for your interest in contributing! This document provides guidelines and instructions for contributing to this project.\n4\t\n5\t## Development Setup\n6\t\n7\t1. **Fork and clone the repository**\n8\t\n9\t   ```bash\n10\t   git clone https://github.com/YOUR-USERNAME/doublets-rs.git\n11\t   cd doublets-rs\n12\t   git submodule update --init --recursive\n13\t   ```\n14\t\n15\t2. **Install Rust**\n16\t\n17\t   Install Rust using rustup (if not already installed):\n18\t\n19\t   ```bash\n20\t   curl --proto '=https' --tlsv1.2 -sSf https://sh.rustup.rs | sh\n21\t   ```\n22\t\n23\t   The project uses a specific nightly toolchain configured in `rust-toolchain.toml`:\n24\t   ```toml\n25\t   [toolchain]\n26\t   channel = \"nightly-2022-08-22\"\n27\t   ```\n28\t\n29\t3. **Install development tools**\n30\t\n31\t   ```bash\n32\t   rustup component add rustfmt clippy\n33\t   ```\n34\t\n35\t4. **Install pre-commit hooks** (optional but recommended)\n36\t\n37\t   ```bash\n38\t   pip install pre-commit\n39\t   pre-commit install\n40\t   ```\n41\t\n42\t5. **Build the project**\n43\t\n44\t   ```bash\n45\t   cargo build\n46\t   ```\n47\t\n48\t## Development Workflow\n49\t\n50\t1. **Create a feature branch**\n51\t\n52\t   ```bash\n53\t   git checkout -b feature/my-feature\n54\t   ```\n55\t\n56\t2. **Make your changes**\n57\t\n58\t   - Write code following the project's style guidelines\n59\t   - Add tests for any new functionality\n60\t   - Update documentation as needed\n61\t\n62\t3. **Run quality checks**\n63\t\n64\t   ```bash\n65\t   # Format code\n66\t   cargo fmt\n67\t\n68\t   # Run Clippy lints\n69\t   cargo clippy --all --tests --all-features\n70\t\n71\t   # Check file sizes\n72\t   node scripts/check-file-size.mjs\n73\t\n74\t   # Run all checks together\n75\t   cargo fmt --check && cargo clippy --all --tests --all-features && node scripts/check-file-size.mjs\n76\t   ```\n77\t\n78\t4. **Run tests**\n79\t\n80\t   ```bash\n81\t   # Run all tests\n82\t   cargo test\n83\t\n84\t   # Run tests with verbose output\n85\t   cargo test --verbose\n86\t\n87\t   # Run doc tests\n88\t   cargo test --doc\n89\t\n90\t   # Run a specific test\n91\t   cargo test test_name\n92\t   ```\n93\t\n94\t5. **Add a changelog fragment**\n95\t\n96\t   For any user-facing changes, create a changelog fragment:\n97\t\n98\t   ```bash\n99\t   # Create a new file in changelog.d/\n100\t   # Format: YYYYMMDD_HHMMSS_description.md\n101\t   touch changelog.d/$(date +%Y%m%d_%H%M%S)_my_change.md\n102\t   ```\n103\t\n104\t   Edit the file to document your changes:\n105\t\n106\t   ```markdown\n107\t   ### Added\n108\t   - Description of new feature\n109\t\n110\t   ### Fixed\n111\t   - Description of bug fix\n112\t   ```\n113\t\n114\t   **Why fragments?** This prevents merge conflicts in CHANGELOG.md when multiple PRs are open simultaneously.\n115\t\n116\t6. **Commit your changes**\n117\t\n118\t   ```bash\n119\t   git add .\n120\t   git commit -m \"feat: add new feature\"\n121\t   ```\n122\t\n123\t   Pre-commit hooks will automatically run and check your code.\n124\t\n125\t7. **Push and create a Pull Request**\n126\t\n127\t   ```bash\n128\t   git push origin feature/my-feature\n129\t   ```\n130\t\n131\t   Then create a Pull Request on GitHub.\n132\t\n133\t## Code Style Guidelines\n134\t\n135\tThis project uses:\n136\t\n137\t- **rustfmt** for code formatting\n138\t- **Clippy** for linting with pedantic and nursery lints enabled\n139\t- **cargo test** for testing\n140\t\n141\t### Code Standards\n142\t\n143\t- Follow Rust idioms and best practices\n144\t- Use documentation comments (`///`) for all public APIs\n145\t- Write tests for all new functionality\n146\t- Keep functions focused and reasonably sized\n147\t- Keep files under 1000 lines\n148\t- Use meaningful variable and function names\n149\t\n150\t### Documentation Format\n151\t\n152\tUse Rust documentation comments:\n153\t\n154\t```rust\n155\t/// Brief description of the function.\n156\t///\n157\t/// Longer description if needed.\n158\t///\n159\t/// # Arguments\n160\t///\n161\t/// * `arg1` - Description of arg1\n162\t/// * `arg2` - Description of arg2\n163\t///\n164\t/// # Returns\n165\t///\n166\t/// Description of return value\n167\t///\n168\t/// # Errors\n169\t///\n170\t/// Description of when errors are returned\n171\t///\n172\t/// # Examples\n173\t///\n174\t/// ```\n175\t/// use doublets::example_function;\n176\t/// let result = example_function(1, 2);\n177\t/// assert_eq!(result, 3);\n178\t/// ```\n179\tpub fn example_function(arg1: i32, arg2: i32) -> i32 {\n180\t    arg1 + arg2\n181\t}\n182\t```\n183\t\n184\t## Testing Guidelines\n185\t\n186\t- Write tests for all new features\n187\t- Maintain or improve test coverage\n188\t- Use descriptive test names\n189\t- Organize tests in modules when appropriate\n190\t- Use `#[cfg(test)]` for test-only code\n191\t\n192\tExample test structure:\n193\t\n194\t```rust\n195\t#[cfg(test)]\n196\tmod tests {\n197\t    use super::*;\n198\t\n199\t    mod my_feature_tests {\n200\t        use super::*;\n201\t\n202\t        #[test]\n203\t        fn test_basic_functionality() {\n204\t            assert_eq!(my_function(), expected_result);\n205\t        }\n206\t\n207\t        #[test]\n208\t        fn test_edge_case() {\n209\t            assert_eq!(my_function(edge_case_input), expected_result);\n210\t        }\n211\t    }\n212\t}\n213\t```\n214\t\n215\t## Pull Request Process\n216\t\n217\t1. Ensure all tests pass locally\n218\t2. Update documentation if needed\n219\t3. Add a changelog fragment (see step 5 in Development Workflow)\n220\t4. Ensure the PR description clearly describes the changes\n221\t5. Link any related issues in the PR description\n222\t6. Wait for CI checks to pass\n223\t7. Address any review feedback\n224\t\n225\t## Changelog Management\n226\t\n227\tThis project uses a fragment-based changelog system similar to [Scriv](https://scriv.readthedocs.io/) (Python) and [Changesets](https://github.com/changesets/changesets) (JavaScript).\n228\t\n229\t### Creating a Fragment\n230\t\n231\t```bash\n232\t# Create a new fragment with timestamp\n233\ttouch changelog.d/$(date +%Y%m%d_%H%M%S)_description.md\n234\t```\n235\t\n236\t### Fragment Categories\n237\t\n238\tUse these categories in your fragments:\n239\t\n240\t- **Added**: New features\n241\t- **Changed**: Changes to existing functionality\n242\t- **Deprecated**: Features that will be removed in future\n243\t- **Removed**: Features that were removed\n244\t- **Fixed**: Bug fixes\n245\t- **Security**: Security-related changes\n246\t\n247\t### During Release\n248\t\n249\tFragments are automatically collected into CHANGELOG.md during the release process. The release workflow:\n250\t\n251\t1. Collects all fragments\n252\t2. Updates CHANGELOG.md with the new version entry\n253\t3. Removes processed fragment files\n254\t4. Bumps the version in Cargo.toml\n255\t5. Creates a git tag and GitHub release\n256\t\n257\t## Project Structure\n258\t\n259\t```\n260\t.\n261\t├── .github/workflows/    # GitHub Actions CI/CD\n262\t├── changelog.d/          # Changelog fragments\n263\t│   └── README.md         # Fragment instructions\n264\t├── ci/                   # CI scripts\n265\t├── dev-deps/             # Development dependencies (git submodules)\n266\t├── doublets/             # Main doublets library\n267\t│   ├── src/              # Source code\n268\t│   ├── tests/            # Integration tests\n269\t│   └── benches/          # Benchmarks\n270\t├── doublets-decorators/  # Decorator patterns (in rework)\n271\t├── doublets-ffi/         # FFI bindings\n272\t├── integration/          # Integration tests\n273\t├── scripts/              # Utility scripts\n274\t├── .gitignore            # Git ignore patterns\n275\t├── .pre-commit-config.yaml  # Pre-commit hooks\n276\t├── Cargo.toml            # Workspace configuration\n277\t├── CHANGELOG.md          # Project changelog\n278\t├── CONTRIBUTING.md       # This file\n279\t├── LICENSE               # Unlicense (public domain)\n280\t├── README.md             # Project README\n281\t└── rust-toolchain.toml   # Rust toolchain configuration\n282\t```\n283\t\n284\t## Release Process\n285\t\n286\tThis project uses semantic versioning (MAJOR.MINOR.PATCH):\n287\t\n288\t- **MAJOR**: Breaking changes\n289\t- **MINOR**: New features (backward compatible)\n290\t- **PATCH**: Bug fixes (backward compatible)\n291\t\n292\tReleases are managed through GitHub releases. To trigger a release:\n293\t\n294\t1. Manually trigger the release workflow with a version bump type\n295\t2. Or: Update the version in Cargo.toml and push to main\n296\t\n297\t## Getting Help\n298\t\n299\t- Open an issue for bugs or feature requests\n300\t- Use discussions for questions and general help\n301\t- Check existing issues and PRs before creating new ones\n302\t\n303\t## Code of Conduct\n304\t\n305\t- Be respectful and inclusive\n306\t- Provide constructive feedback\n307\t- Focus on what is best for the community\n308\t- Show empathy towards other community members\n309\t\n310\tThank you for contributing!\n311\t"
